Karma (@karma: 0, is #chapter, #instinct, #done) { r_K7T3ZG08G5EBWRSTF8NBWMTZYV
Karma means 'action' in Sanskrit. In Lince it is the sum of all automations and intelligence. Without Karma in Lince, all the data is like still water if not touched by the user. Karma is the scheduler, the automatic action taker, it changes quantites, fires computer commands, receives requests from other systems and saves the result to be used in the Rules. All of it is optional, it can be set to take every Fact as an evidence that a certain Karma Rule should happen in such and such way. A pattern can be understood and have a growth function, so that after 3 months of buying apples every week you create an automation around posting a need for apples and a transfer proposal for such, all with hands-on control or complete automation. 

Rules are meant to be math equations that pull valus from your data and other systems to compute Conditions. These Conditions are checked to see if they pass a Threshold, if they do they enact a Consequence.

Everything up to here you do by hand. **Karma** is the part of Lince that does things without you — it watches Records, does arithmetic on them, and changes them, on a beat or when something moves, with your permission and a readable trail. Records model the world and Transfer coordinates change between people; Karma turns evidence into action.

It is useful without an LLM. People author exact rules visually, use transparent statistics and deterministic algorithms, and plan over their own goals. A model may be attached as an explicit Signal or author an inert candidate; **model access is optional and grants no special authority.** "Always on" means a supervised runtime keeps draining durable work — never always interrupting, always connected, or always authorized to act.

A rule is one sentence long, and the parts below take that sentence apart: what it watches, when it counts as true, and what it does about it.

## One Rule in One Sentence

Everything up to here you do by hand. **Karma** is how Lince does things without you. And it is one sentence long:

```mermaid
graph LR
  A["If: Condition"] --> B["And: Threshold"] --> C["Then: Consequence"]
```

In ordinary language:

```mermaid
graph LR
  A["If: tomorrow is cold"] --> B["And: cold enough to matter"] --> C["Then: need to pack a sweater"]
```

And when the world says otherwise, the same rule simply does nothing:

```mermaid
graph LR
  A["If: tomorrow is mild"] --> B["And: not cold enough"] --> C["Then: nothing happens"]
```

## Condition

The condition is an equation, and it can draw on three kinds of input:

```mermaid
graph LR
  A["A Frequency"] --> Z["Condition"]
  B["A shell command"] --> Z
  C["A record's quantity"] --> Z
```

Each contributes a number, and the ranges matter more than they look:

- A **Frequency** gives `0` or `1` — it is the clock. It sits on a **Cadence** ("monthly until June", "every 14th"), and stays 0 until the scheduled instant arrives.

- A **command** gives whatever it prints, read as a number.

- A **record's quantity** gives anything at all, from very negative to very positive.

So a condition is arithmetic over live data. Write it abstractly and then substitute what is actually true right now:

```mermaid
graph TD
  A["Condition: Data > 5"] --> B["Condition: that record's quantity > 5"]
  B --> C["Condition: 2 > 5"]
  C --> D["Condition: false"]
```

_The same rule, resolved one step at a time against the world as it is._

# Tasks


- [ ] **Drop `value(@slug)` and `derived_value`.** Reading another rule's number without running it needs recursion, a depth cap, cycle detection and transitive wake-up that `reads()` cannot express. Chaining through committed Facts already does the useful half.
- [ ] **Refuse a unit mismatch at publish time**, not at first run. Conversion is exact and explicit, never an implicit coercion to make an expression type-check.
- [ ] **Read a number out of a namespaced Record extension**, so a rule can use a Record's weight, price and count and not only its quantity. A missing one is an error, never a silent zero.
- [ ] **A saved Protein view is a block**, reducing to exactly one number from exactly one row. A view that fails to execute blocks its rule as a refusal, never as an error to be logged. The legacy engine had this; §1.2 currently does not.

## Threshold

The threshold is the guard between having a number and doing something about it. The usual one is simply "not zero".

```mermaid
graph LR
  A["Condition: 0 (false)"] --> B["Threshold: not 0"] --> C["stops here"]
```

```mermaid
graph LR
  A["Condition: 2 (a quantity)"] --> B["Threshold: not 0"] --> C["Consequence runs"]
```

_This is why a Frequency returning 0 is enough to keep a whole rule quiet._

#### In detail

**Purpose:** Decide whether the number the Condition produced means *fire*, kept deliberately small so the interesting part stays in the arithmetic.

**How it works:** *only non-zero*, *anything*, or a comparison against a number. That is the whole vocabulary. When it passes, the Condition's value is carried into the Consequence.

### What is left

#### 1.3 — Threshold

- [ ] **Specify each gate's transition behaviour** — edge or level trigger, hysteresis, and what a re-fire on an unchanged reading means. A threshold exposes *entered* and *left* as two distinct events, not one boolean.
- [ ] **Debounce:** a reading must hold for a declared span before it counts, so a value flickering across the line does not fire ten times.
- [ ] **Cooldown:** after firing, refuse for a declared span.
- [ ] **Rate limit:** at most `max` firings in a rolling window. These three are temporal controls on the Threshold, not new rule kinds, and each needs its state persisted so a restart does not forget a cooldown.

## Consequence

```mermaid
graph LR
  A["Consequence"] --> B["Change a record's quantity"]
  A --> C["Run a shell command"]
  A --> D["Run SQL"]
```

The first one is the interesting one, because a changed quantity is itself something another rule can be watching. Rules chain without ever referring to each other:

```mermaid
graph TD
  A["Condition: record reaches a value"] --> B["Consequence: another record changes"]
  B --> C["Condition: that record changed"]
  C --> D["Consequence: a command runs"]
```

_Nobody wired these two rules together. They meet in the data._

#### In detail

**Purpose:** The thing that actually happens, receiving the number the Condition carried.

**How it works:** An ordered, non-empty list — capture an entry, set or add a quantity, assert or retract an ordinary Record assertion, or set an identity assertion. A list rather than one, because `@wip → @done` is one intention and must be one rule.

**Interacts:** A consequence appends an ordinary signed Fact on the target Record, which is a Record moving, which wakes any rule reading it. Anything leaving the Cell goes through §5's grant and §6's intent instead of firing directly.

### What is left

#### 1.4 — Consequence

- [x] **Shell Command as a consequence** — declared executable identity and hash, typed arguments, no string interpolation, timeout, output capture.
- [/] **A computed Record refuses conflicting writes.** If a Record's quantity is defined by a rule, a manual write is a refusal with a reason, not a silent overwrite.
- [x] **Cycles are discovered at proof time**, not at runtime.
      - [ ] After interface refactor the karma sand should give the user the notice about the cycle when they eventually create one.
- [/] **A consequence has a route: none, propose, or apply.** *Propose* puts a draft in front of a person; *apply* must name a narrow grant (§5). Without one, time never changes a quantity by itself. The route is a field on the consequence, not a different kind of rule. Five routes exist as a field on the node — observe, recommend, draft, ask, act — and the gate is real: a candidate is inert until a person responds, and only an accepted one naming an active grant becomes an intent. What is missing is the other side of the gate: an authorized intent never runs, so *apply* is a door with nothing behind it.
      - [ ] **Execute an authorized intent.** Until this lands, `karma_intent_not_implemented` and `karma_execution_not_implemented` are what the capability list reports, and time changes no quantity by any route.
- [ ] **Ask, notify and emit-promise** as consequences, so a rule can raise a question (§10) or open a Promise instead of only moving a number. This needs more explanation.

## Frequency

```mermaid
graph LR
  A["-1 * (Frequency * record quantity)"] --> B["Threshold"] --> C["another record's quantity"]
```

For most of the month the Frequency is `0`, so the whole equation is `0`, so the threshold stops it and nothing happens. On the scheduled day the Frequency becomes `1`, the equation takes the value of the first record, and that amount moves into the second. A monthly bill, with no scheduler and no special "recurring" feature — just a number that is zero most of the time.

- [x] **A step is a sum of components** applied largest unit first — years and months against the anchor's own day-of-month (a too-short month clamps or skips per `invalid_day`), then weeks through milliseconds as an exact duration, optionally rolled forward onto an allowed weekday. So `1 month + 1 day + 1 second + 10 milliseconds, then forward to Friday` is one step, and every component is typeable in the sand.
- [x] **The slug is the only way in.** Charset validated, zero step refused, creation idempotent by `request_id`, deletion refused while a rule's `condition_src` still names it.
- [x] **`freq(@x)` reads the table with `(since, at]` edges**, falling back to the old rule-carried rhythm so rules written before it keep working.

### What is left

#### 1.5 — Frequency

There are two schedulers in the tree, and every box below is about the second one. The first is the one a person can actually reach: a rule is a Recurrence carrying a `condition_src`, the sand writes it with `create-recurrence`, `freq(@x)` reads the `frequency` table the sand fills with `create-frequency`, and the whole thing is looked at by `fire_due_rules` inside a sixty-second heartbeat poll. The second is the Program: a proven graph of nodes, its own Frequency object with a compiled cadence, armed cursors, a deadline director, occurrences, epochs, runs and candidates. The second is where the work below landed. Nothing in the interface authors a Program or its Frequency, and until that changes the director wakes for schedules only a test can create, while every rule a person writes is still polled once a minute.

- [x] **One director, one sleep, one wake per earliest deadline.** Not one loop that polls every frequency, and not one task per frequency that merely exists. Every active Frequency arms a cursor in a deadline index; the director sleeps to the earliest of them, wakes, claims and completes exactly the cursors that came due, rearms them, and sleeps again. A `1s` and a `1d` frequency still cost one wake each per their own period, which is the entire point, and one task rather than a task each is what makes ten thousand of them a wake and not ten thousand threads. This is in line with Resenha, that can push time over many years: the clock is a trait the runtime is handed, so Resenha drives the same director with its own, and every next instant is computed `next_on_or_after` from the cadence anchor rather than accumulated from the last wake, so advancing 100 years lands exactly where a second-by-second run would. Drift cannot accumulate because nothing adds up; a wake that arrives late is still measured against the anchor, and a clock that jumps is a typed discontinuity that rebuilds the index rather than a silently skewed schedule. It runs on a real Cell now — it was reachable only from tests before, so every activation failed for want of an installed runtime.
- [x] **Lifecycle.** A cursor is armed when an activation becomes active and superseded when its revision changes or it stops; the director rebuilds its index on a change signal rather than holding a handle per frequency. Leases are persisted and expire, so a Cell that dies mid-beat is recovered by the next one rather than losing the beat.
- [x] **Reuse is the point** — ten rules reading one Frequency wake from one timer, not ten. One cursor per Frequency, and one beat opens one epoch that every active Program is a member of.
- [/] **Civil resolution at fire time.** The read path takes a timezone provider, content-addressed by tzdb revision, and a Frequency naming a revision no installed provider serves is refused when it is activated rather than silently never firing. But the only provider shipped is an in-process UTC one (`lince-utc.1`), so a rule that must land on a local Friday still cannot be activated at all.
      - [ ] **Ship a real tzdb artifact**, or generate one at build time, so civil time is a choice and not a refusal.
- [ ] **Put the rules people write on the director.** A beat proves out end to end as far as a candidate — occurrence, epoch, run, proposal — and stops there, for Programs nobody can author. The sixty-second heartbeat stays until a Recurrence either becomes a Program or reads the same cursors.
- [ ] **The interface authors none of this.** No sand calls create-karma-program, create-karma-frequency, activate-karma-frequency, respond-karma-candidate, or any of the grant actions; the Karma sand's frequency form writes the old table instead. Everything in §1.5 and §1.4's route is API-only until a Sand reaches it.

- [ ] **Missed beats on boot.** `between(anchor, last_fired, now)` gives them; fire those, then enter the sleep loop. Safe every boot because firing is keyed `<rule_uid>:<frequency_uid>:<beat_at>` on `entry_revision`'s existing `UNIQUE(request_id)`.
- [ ] **`freq(@x)` becomes a beat indicator** — 1 on the beat, 0 otherwise — once the timer tasks land, and the `since` plumbing goes with it.
- [ ] **Projection is bounded.** Five years of a monthly Frequency is sixty evaluations; a `1s` Frequency over the same span is not a query. Refuse with the count rather than hanging.
- [ ] **The missed policy is declared, not assumed** — fire every missed beat, coalesce them into one, take only the latest, or skip to the next anchor. A Cell asleep three weeks owes three Mondays only if it said so.
- [ ] **Changing a step declares its rephase policy** — keep the anchor, or re-anchor from now. Silently doing either is a bug in one direction or the other.
- [ ] **Admission: a Frequency the Cell cannot serve is refused at activation, not discovered at runtime.** A `1ms` beat is a real request and a real cost; say no with the arithmetic, and offer a shadow run.

## The whole picture

```mermaid
graph LR
  A["Frequency"] --> B["Condition"]
  C["Command"] --> B
  D["SQL"] --> B
  E["Record"] --> B
  B --> F["Threshold"]
  F --> G["Record"]
  F --> H["Command"]
  F --> I["SQL"]
```

_The same kinds of thing go in and come out — which is exactly why rules compose._

Notice what is missing: there is no list of automations Lince supports. There is one shape — if, and, then — pointed at records you already have. A budget that refills, a habit that nags, a stock level that reorders, and a Transfer that settles itself are the same machine with different numbers in it.

## 18 — What this must be able to do

**Purpose:** The list of things a person should be able to build with the
sections above. It exists so a redesign that simplifies the machinery cannot
quietly drop a capability — if one of these stops being expressible, the
simplification went too far.

Each is one vertical someone actually wants, and each is a test as much as a
feature. None require domain-specific backend code; they differ only in which
Records exist and what they are called. **These are bullets, not checkboxes:
they are what to test §1–§16 against, not work to schedule on their own.**

### What is left

- [ ] **A percentage always includes numerator, denominator and cohort definition.** A bare percentage is not a shareable product.
- [ ] **Sharing a live rule means sharing a content-hashed definition**, not a running process.
- [ ] **Revocation stops future export and use and cancels eligible queued work.**
- [ ] **Enumerate every declared source port and every reachable outcome path** before it happens.
- [ ] **Make every "why" navigable in both directions** — changed Fact → the run that changed it → the rule → the grant, and back down.
- [ ] **Make data scope and authority visible on the graph** — taint paths and ceilings drawn, not documented.
- [ ] **Dry-run one node or a whole rule** against current or simulated inputs.
- [ ] **Store canvas layout as host state**, never as a Ledger Fact.
- [ ] **Large graphs** use server-projected dependency slices and stable ids.
- [ ] **Installable templates are ordinary disabled rules**, not a second format.
- [ ] **Every control is also a typed Action**, and Protein exposes capability booleans with stable blocking reasons beside every one of them, so an agent can do exactly what a person can do and nothing more.

# 1 — Rule

**Purpose:** A Rule is the whole of Karma's authoring surface. It is three parts and nothing else, and it is one object so a person never has to learn where the schedule went.

**How it works:** The Condition computes a number from blocks referenced by slug. The Threshold decides whether that number means *fire*. The Consequence happens and receives the number the Condition carried.

```
-1 * freq(@daily) + @apple    →  !=0  →  add-quantity @pear
└──────── Condition ────────┘    Thresh.   └── Consequence ──┘
```

**Interacts:** Reads Record quantities and Frequencies; writes through Actions, which append Facts, which wake other Rules — that chain is deliberate and is how one Rule's consequence feeds another's condition.

With #fruit, a rule keeps catching Records asserted after it was written — you don't revisit the rule. Renaming a concept is free: rules keep working and show the new name at once. #fruta and #Frucht write the same rule — each person reads it in their own name for it, and naming two aliases of one concept twice in one rule is refused as the duplicate it is. Writing a rule with a name that means two concepts is refused there and then, so you say which. (And: reusing a deleted @slug points old rules at the new Record.)

- [ ] Right now a rule only produces a suggestion sitting in a list, waiting for you to press a button. Default to all created rules by People be always picked up from the list automatically.
- [ ] Timeline: Facts are the past, Protein is the present, the future must be some gradient between past statistics and future projections with Frequency simulating what will happen.
- [ ] Instead of Commands immediatelly being the calling of some bash and putting it's value in some Condition we save it as a Signal, only then it is used in the Conditon.
- [ ] Being able to accept commands over some endpoint, with some form of access control. so /command/slug is not callable by anyone.


